<header><p class="st-kicker">other · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Carausian Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">286 CE – 296 CE · capital Londinium (London)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Breakaway Roman regime in Britain and northern Gaul, founded by the admiral Carausius in 286, the first time Britain was ruled as a separate realm.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carausius">Carausius</a>, a Menapian from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, commanded the Roman fleet charged with clearing Frankish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">Channel</a>. Accused of keeping captured plunder for himself and sentenced to death by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a>, he declared himself emperor in 286, taking control of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and part of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> with his fleet.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carausius">Carausius</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-boulogne">Boulogne</a>, presenting himself as a legitimate colleague of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a>. His coins, minted at London and another British mint, carried slogans such as RESTITVTOR BRITANNIAE and EXPECTATE VENI, quoting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>. He kept a strong fleet, repelled Maximian's attempted invasion in 289 and may have begun forts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> Shore.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 293 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-i">Constantius Chlorus</a> captured <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-boulogne">Boulogne</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carausius">Carausius</a> was murdered by his finance minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allectus">Allectus</a>, who took power. In 296 Constantius invaded; his prefect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-asclepiodotus">Asclepiodotus</a> defeated and killed Allectus, and Constantius entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> medallion celebrates. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> returned to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/carausian-empire.jpg" alt="Carausian Empire" width="320" height="147"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoninianus_Carausius_leg4-RIC_0069v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>286 CE – 296 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Londinium (London)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Carausius, Allectus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom, France</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Britannic Empire, Imperium Britanniarum</dd></dl></aside></div>
